WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.480
Jay Armstrong: I go to the psych ward. They were like,
"Mr. Armstrong, you've been here a ton of

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:08.069
times. What could you possibly say to us
to make us think you're serious about

00:00:08.069 --> 00:00:11.940
staying clean this time?" And I was like,
"I'm not here to detox. I have a plan

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.960
to step in front of a train."

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.939
Justin Ponton: Well I found
myself at the Huntington City Mission,

00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:28.640
homeless, on parole, completely addicted
to heroin.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.570
Kelvin Young: I have a daughter. You know, she

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:35.730
seen me under the influence of heroin at a
public event and how I was so out of my

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:41.220
mind that I'd even actually urinate on myself.
My daughter seen that.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:50.200
Justin Ponton: I went to my parole officer and I cried
to her and I told her, "I don't have

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:55.130
anyone left at all." She said, "You got me
JP," and she reached across the table and

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:59.180
she gave me a fist bump. All that
mattered was I at least I have one person,

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.700
and that gave me some hope.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.739
Kelvin Young: It really
took me going to prison for the fourth

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:08.990
time to really do the inner work I needed to do, you know,
to find freedom. I'll never forget laying on

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:14.950
a bunk bed and saying to myself, "I needed to make some changes in my life."

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:20.810
Sally Satel: Recovery from addiction takes place in
basically two phases. The first phase

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:25.540
entails what I call stabilizing a
patient. This often, but not always,

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:32.060
involves medication. So the first step of
recovery is stabilization. At least the

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:36.290
person is standing still, working on
getting the rest of his or her life in

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:42.500
order. But then you have to draw the lens
out further. So you've got someone who's

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.490
stable, they're certainly not in
withdrawal, but their life isn't really

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:50.660
much better, and they don't see much of a
future for themselves, and someone like

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:54.060
that is at risk for relapsing.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:11.560
Kelvin Young: So I took advantage of every single
program available while I was in prison.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:16.120
That really helped me to deal with a lot of my
raw, human emotion that I suppressed

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.680
with alcohol and other drugs, and do the
inner work I needed to do in order to heal.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.709
Matt Peterson: Once I got out, started working, met a

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:26.800
great girl. I was a cook at a really nice seafood restaurant.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.470
Jay Armstrong: I've been doing stand-up for
just over eight years now.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:32.400
I got into a kind of on a whim. I've
always wanted to do it.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.400
Justin Ponton: After I got myself together and I got sober,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.560
I developed and created a sober-living program.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.520
Jay Armstrong: The thing about medically-assisted
treatment is that there are some people

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:52.010
that that is as good as it's gonna get
for them. I don't see it helping the

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:56.209
epidemic because these doctors that
prescribe it that's their go-to now.

00:02:56.209 --> 00:03:00.890
There's no, "Hey, can we try to get this
person, you know, regular treatment first?"

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:04.970
Justin Ponton: Fortunately, the route that I was
given an opportunity to take did not

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:10.310
involve replacing one substance with
another. I don't want to just be

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:14.580
clean and sober. To me, that's... that's not
recovery.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.120
Kelvin Young: One program that helped me the most

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:19.579
was a drug treatment program within
the prison. So after I finished the

00:03:19.579 --> 00:03:23.359
program, they hired me to be a peer
mentor and helping other inmates that

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.980
was incarcerated with me to deal with
their recovery, and it gave me a sense of purpose.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:34.640
Sally Satel: A very important part of
treatment and rehabilitation is getting

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:41.120
a person back to work. Work is so
important as therapy. It provides a sense

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.540
of purpose. It keeps people busy.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:57.450
Matt Peterson: Well, I'm a full-time father. I work
full-time as a peer recovery specialist.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:00.340
Kelvin Young: Today I'm a certified addiction recovery
coach.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.480
Justin Ponton: My life is entirely all about

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:05.560
family and work.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:08.060
Jay Armstrong: You know, everyone expected me to die a junkie's death.

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:11.340
But today I'm a productive
member of society. I'm a father, and I do

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.730
my best to try to lead those kids down a
way to where they know that they can

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:16.880
talk about anything that's going on with
them

